1
00:00:02,281 --> 00:00:04,418
What's this CORS policy?

2
00:00:05,012 --> 00:00:08,547
CORS stands for Cross-Origin Resource

3
00:00:08,547 --> 00:00:08,916
Sharing.

4
00:00:09,655 --> 00:00:10,812
And here's what it's all about.

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:15,054
We have 2 servers and we typically do if we

6
00:00:15,054 --> 00:00:18,652
have a REST API and a frontend. One server

7
00:00:18,845 --> 00:00:21,721
serves the frontend, in our case, the React

8
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:23,135
single page application.

9
00:00:23,729 --> 00:00:26,412
The other server serves our REST API.

10
00:00:27,263 --> 00:00:30,232
Now we want our frontend app to talk to the

11
00:00:30,232 --> 00:00:33,200
REST API to fetch data or to send data.

12
00:00:33,826 --> 00:00:35,976
But the thing you have to know is that

13
00:00:35,992 --> 00:00:40,084
modern browsers by default don't allow this

14
00:00:40,084 --> 00:00:43,357
interaction. They block requests being sent

15
00:00:43,694 --> 00:00:45,796
from the client to the server. To be

16
00:00:45,796 --> 00:00:48,428
precise, they don't block it, but they won't

17
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:50,690
return the response by the server.

18
00:00:51,412 --> 00:00:53,851
This is a security mechanism to make sure

19
00:00:54,381 --> 00:00:57,429
that your JavaScript code that runs in the

20
00:00:57,429 --> 00:01:00,204
browser is only able to talk to your own

21
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,578
server and not to some other server which

22
00:01:02,866 --> 00:01:03,139
could be

23
00:01:03,668 --> 00:01:05,143
used to do something malicious.

24
00:01:05,913 --> 00:01:08,255
Now, of course, when we build an API, we

25
00:01:08,255 --> 00:01:11,911
typically do want to expose it to all kinds

26
00:01:11,943 --> 00:01:14,589
of servers though. We don't want to lock it

27
00:01:14,589 --> 00:01:16,963
down to just the server on which the API

28
00:01:17,075 --> 00:01:18,999
runs because that would mean that

29
00:01:18,999 --> 00:01:21,646
essentially no one is able to use our API.

30
00:01:21,886 --> 00:01:23,939
So we wanna circumvent the security

31
00:01:23,939 --> 00:01:27,162
mechanism which exists for a good reason but

32
00:01:27,162 --> 00:01:28,349
which we don't always want.

33
00:01:29,102 --> 00:01:31,603
And the good thing is we can disable this

34
00:01:31,747 --> 00:01:32,757
CORS checking.

35
00:01:33,495 --> 00:01:36,637
On the server, we can set specific response

36
00:01:36,637 --> 00:01:39,522
headers to the responses we're sending back

37
00:01:39,522 --> 00:01:42,343
to the client to essentially let the browser

38
00:01:42,343 --> 00:01:45,149
know that we're fine with returning that

39
00:01:45,149 --> 00:01:47,697
information and that the browser should get

40
00:01:47,842 --> 00:01:49,765
out of the way and not block it.

41
00:01:50,599 --> 00:01:54,302
So we can configure our server to allow this

42
00:01:54,574 --> 00:01:54,975
access.

43
00:01:55,633 --> 00:01:57,287
And it's really important that this is only

44
00:01:57,319 --> 00:01:58,250
a browser thing.

45
00:01:58,828 --> 00:02:01,220
In Postman, we didn't have any problems

46
00:02:01,412 --> 00:02:04,029
because Postman doesn't have this security

47
00:02:04,061 --> 00:02:04,623
concept.

48
00:02:05,169 --> 00:02:07,192
So it's really just a thing if you have a

49
00:02:07,192 --> 00:02:08,011
browser web app.

50
00:02:08,797 --> 00:02:12,891
But in that case, on the server, in app.ts

51
00:02:12,891 --> 00:02:16,230
for example, you want to set headers that

52
00:02:16,262 --> 00:02:19,602
grant the client access to your resources.

53
00:02:20,517 --> 00:02:21,608
And that can be done

54
00:02:22,282 --> 00:02:23,598
by adding a middleware here

55
00:02:24,288 --> 00:02:26,951
in the app.ts file, now back in the Deno

56
00:02:27,079 --> 00:02:29,598
code. So this is now not the frontend, this

57
00:02:29,598 --> 00:02:30,480
is the Deno code.

58
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:32,919
Here we have a regular middleware with

59
00:02:33,031 --> 00:02:34,202
context and next,

60
00:02:35,181 --> 00:02:37,828
and we now want to reach out to the response

61
00:02:37,828 --> 00:02:39,096
on the context object

62
00:02:39,673 --> 00:02:42,384
and to the headers there and call set to set

63
00:02:42,417 --> 00:02:43,219
a new header.

64
00:02:44,085 --> 00:02:47,149
And now there are 3 headers which we wanna

65
00:02:47,149 --> 00:02:49,918
set here. The first header is the

66
00:02:50,111 --> 00:02:55,947
Access-Control-Allow-Origin header. And make

67
00:02:55,947 --> 00:02:58,632
sure you have no typo, all the dashes are

68
00:02:58,680 --> 00:02:59,212
important.

69
00:02:59,890 --> 00:03:03,134
Here, you basically set which origins, so

70
00:03:03,263 --> 00:03:05,941
which other domains are allowed to access

71
00:03:05,941 --> 00:03:06,506
this API.

72
00:03:07,055 --> 00:03:09,062
You could restrict this to some domain

73
00:03:09,741 --> 00:03:12,266
.com, but I will set it to a wildcard

74
00:03:12,412 --> 00:03:15,195
character to grant any server access to this

75
00:03:15,389 --> 00:03:15,810
API.

76
00:03:17,268 --> 00:03:19,965
The next header which we need to set here

77
00:03:20,563 --> 00:03:25,585
is the Access-Control-Allow-Methods header.

78
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:29,082
We defined which domains may access the API.

79
00:03:29,677 --> 00:03:32,479
Now we need to define which HTTP methods

80
00:03:32,737 --> 00:03:35,169
should be allowed, so which kind of requests

81
00:03:35,410 --> 00:03:36,070
can be sent.

82
00:03:36,779 --> 00:03:39,274
And here I want to allow GET, POST,

83
00:03:39,919 --> 00:03:40,208
PATCH,

84
00:03:41,110 --> 00:03:43,679
and DELETE. All separated by commas.

85
00:03:44,436 --> 00:03:46,417
And I'm simply going with these methods

86
00:03:46,691 --> 00:03:48,881
because these are the methods I use in my

87
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:49,332
routes.

88
00:03:49,911 --> 00:03:51,991
I'm not allowing PUT requests, for example,

89
00:03:52,024 --> 00:03:53,662
because we have no PUT route.

90
00:03:55,523 --> 00:03:57,381
The last header that should be sent here

91
00:03:59,143 --> 00:04:04,541
is the Access-Control-Allow-Headers

92
00:04:05,937 --> 00:04:06,280
header.

93
00:04:06,999 --> 00:04:09,970
This defines which headers the client may

94
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:12,773
set on requests it sends to the server.

95
00:04:13,366 --> 00:04:16,026
And here I wanna allow for the Content-Type

96
00:04:16,075 --> 00:04:16,331
header.

97
00:04:16,956 --> 00:04:19,680
I do allow this because in the frontend app,

98
00:04:20,241 --> 00:04:22,421
in the resources folder, in the components

99
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:25,097
folder, and there in the resources.js file,

100
00:04:26,091 --> 00:04:28,992
there on some requests, for example, on the

101
00:04:29,056 --> 00:04:31,988
POST request or the PATCH request for adding

102
00:04:32,117 --> 00:04:35,178
or updating data, I do set the content type

103
00:04:35,178 --> 00:04:39,136
to application/json. And we need to send

104
00:04:39,328 --> 00:04:40,867
this content type here

105
00:04:41,573 --> 00:04:45,119
to ensure that in our backend controller, so

106
00:04:45,119 --> 00:04:48,136
the controller on our server, this body

107
00:04:48,136 --> 00:04:51,281
method is able to extract and parse the JSON

108
00:04:51,281 --> 00:04:51,666
data.

109
00:04:52,308 --> 00:04:55,132
Without the application/json content-type

110
00:04:55,132 --> 00:04:55,501
header,

111
00:04:56,255 --> 00:04:56,544
body

112
00:04:57,282 --> 00:05:00,154
would extract the body but not convert it

113
00:05:00,186 --> 00:05:03,219
from JSON, which is text, to a JavaScript

114
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,819
object. It would just yield us the text

115
00:05:05,867 --> 00:05:08,418
instead and we would have to manually parse

116
00:05:08,531 --> 00:05:08,595
it.

117
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:12,308
So we need this extra Content-Type header to

118
00:05:12,389 --> 00:05:14,989
give our backend, in this case the body

119
00:05:14,989 --> 00:05:18,121
method, a hint on how to parse the request

120
00:05:18,137 --> 00:05:18,508
body.

121
00:05:19,428 --> 00:05:22,541
We didn't need to do this on Postman because

122
00:05:22,654 --> 00:05:23,251
Postman

123
00:05:23,799 --> 00:05:25,945
simply did this automatically. If you

124
00:05:25,945 --> 00:05:27,913
inspect the headers on the POST request, you

125
00:05:27,913 --> 00:05:31,979
see that Content-Type was set automatically

126
00:05:32,076 --> 00:05:32,205
there.

127
00:05:33,866 --> 00:05:35,786
Now it's not set automatically by the

128
00:05:35,786 --> 00:05:37,924
frontend. Here it is because I added the

129
00:05:37,924 --> 00:05:38,882
respective code, but therefore we also need

130
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:43,395
to allow the frontend to set this header

131
00:05:43,395 --> 00:05:44,469
here with this

132
00:05:45,142 --> 00:05:45,735
CORS header.

133
00:05:46,985 --> 00:05:49,244
And with all of that, we can call next to

134
00:05:49,244 --> 00:05:52,305
ensure that the request doesn't die here but

135
00:05:52,353 --> 00:05:53,619
instead reaches the routes.

136
00:05:54,356 --> 00:05:56,583
Since we do asynchronous things in those

137
00:05:56,615 --> 00:06:00,108
routes, we also want to use async here on

138
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,432
this middleware function so that we can

139
00:06:02,464 --> 00:06:05,092
await next here. Otherwise, the response

140
00:06:05,092 --> 00:06:07,191
would be sent back immediately. Now we're

141
00:06:07,191 --> 00:06:09,325
saying, don't just continue to the next

142
00:06:09,357 --> 00:06:11,813
middleware but also wait for it to complete

143
00:06:12,006 --> 00:06:14,029
so that all the route handling completes

144
00:06:14,254 --> 00:06:16,726
before this middleware finishes and we

145
00:06:16,886 --> 00:06:18,893
ultimately send back the response therefore.

146
00:06:20,274 --> 00:06:23,099
And with this changed, if we quit our Deno

147
00:06:23,099 --> 00:06:24,592
server and restart it,

148
00:06:25,652 --> 00:06:26,599
we should be able

149
00:06:27,241 --> 00:06:29,360
to reload our frontend app here

150
00:06:30,292 --> 00:06:32,443
and now it's loading and then we see no

151
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:35,030
error but instead this message. And now we

152
00:06:35,030 --> 00:06:36,754
can add a new learning resource, for

153
00:06:36,754 --> 00:06:38,397
example, Deno the course.

154
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:39,879
This is a

155
00:06:40,685 --> 00:06:42,103
great Deno course,

156
00:06:44,894 --> 00:06:46,219
and here I just link to

157
00:06:46,546 --> 00:06:47,331
https://acadlink/deno.

158
00:06:50,810 --> 00:06:53,640
And then for the image URL, I'm again going

159
00:06:53,737 --> 00:06:54,983
to go with

160
00:06:55,517 --> 00:06:55,792
this

161
00:06:57,053 --> 00:06:59,221
image address here. You can pick any image

162
00:06:59,415 --> 00:07:00,776
URL you want, of course.

163
00:07:01,478 --> 00:07:02,185
Save this,

164
00:07:02,811 --> 00:07:03,277
and here it is.

165
00:07:03,871 --> 00:07:05,284
Here is the course, and this is being

166
00:07:05,381 --> 00:07:07,019
fetched from the database. And hence, if I

167
00:07:07,083 --> 00:07:08,801
reload, it's also loaded from there.

168
00:07:09,412 --> 00:07:11,323
We can click Visit Resource to go to that

169
00:07:11,323 --> 00:07:13,250
link, but we can also edit it.

170
00:07:14,069 --> 00:07:16,012
For example, add a couple of exclamation

171
00:07:16,012 --> 00:07:17,827
marks also down there and save this.

172
00:07:18,951 --> 00:07:20,011
And we can also delete it,

173
00:07:20,573 --> 00:07:22,066
which I want to show you by adding a new

174
00:07:22,243 --> 00:07:22,709
resource,

175
00:07:23,929 --> 00:07:25,246
test dummy,

176
00:07:25,953 --> 00:07:28,008
and now just some dummy URLs.

177
00:07:29,859 --> 00:07:30,311
For both

178
00:07:32,136 --> 00:07:32,765
image and

179
00:07:33,718 --> 00:07:36,253
the resource. If I now delete this, you see

180
00:07:36,253 --> 00:07:36,689
it's gone.

181
00:07:37,450 --> 00:07:39,495
So this now works, and this is now how a

182
00:07:39,495 --> 00:07:42,331
frontend could connect to a backend.

183
00:07:42,943 --> 00:07:46,149
This is a React application talking to our

184
00:07:46,230 --> 00:07:47,830
Deno REST API here,

185
00:07:49,091 --> 00:07:51,500
and this hopefully also makes the difference

186
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:53,989
between server-side rendered views and APIs

187
00:07:54,406 --> 00:07:55,656
100% clear.

188
00:07:56,249 --> 00:07:58,814
Here we have an API which is only about the

189
00:07:58,894 --> 00:08:00,289
data that's being exchanged.

190
00:08:00,898 --> 00:08:04,072
We're not sending or generating any HTML

191
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:06,701
code on the Deno server here. Instead, for

192
00:08:06,749 --> 00:08:09,394
this we have a standalone frontend, a React

193
00:08:09,394 --> 00:08:11,317
single-page application that doesn't know

194
00:08:11,478 --> 00:08:12,552
anything about Deno.

195
00:08:13,177 --> 00:08:15,149
This is responsible for what we're seeing on

196
00:08:15,245 --> 00:08:15,678
the screen.

197
00:08:16,207 --> 00:08:18,194
Everything you're seeing here is rendered

198
00:08:18,291 --> 00:08:21,160
and generated by the React application So

199
00:08:21,176 --> 00:08:23,360
therefore by JavaScript code that runs in

200
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:23,906
the browser.

201
00:08:24,629 --> 00:08:27,149
Only the data that's being used to populate

202
00:08:27,149 --> 00:08:29,799
this, that data is coming from Deno.
